# Target C-II — collapsed support-six blocks consume at least seven strict rhombi

**Date:** 2026-09-19  
**Claim class:** exact finite/global train-topology strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** degree-pure collapsed support-six one-anchor components after maximal two-anchor activation  
**Depends on:** audited collapsed support-six rotation-cell census; audited MI.1 mod-2 relation-cycle equation; stopped-train pseudoline theorem; strict unit-rhombus crossing-angle criterion  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_support6_collapsed_seven_crossing_floor_2026_09_19.cpp`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The remaining degree-pure support-six one-anchor mechanism is the collapsed block

\[
y_1=\cdots=y_6\ne0,
\qquad
R[K]=K_6\setminus M,
\]

with six projectively parallel outside anchors in a `3+3` orientation split. The existing rotation-cell theorem reduced its local angle/order data to 388 exact rational cells, or 42 conservative symmetry orbits. The coarse MI.1 regression then showed that forgetting simultaneous crossing-threshold information is too weak: every one of the 388 coarse parity systems is consistent.

Retaining that information gives a much stronger global conclusion.

Let `X_K` be the number of strict-rhombus crossings whose two complete trains are both among the twelve internal relation trains of the collapsed block `K`. Then

\[
\boxed{X_K\ge7.}
\tag{SC.1}
\]

The proof is finite and exact. It does not use the nonlinear six-pentagon closure equations. For every proposed crossing set of size at most six it simultaneously checks:

1. the full audited MI.1 system over `F_2`;
2. exact joint feasibility of all selected strict crossing windows under one projective-angle vector in the rotation cell; and
3. planarity of the train drawing after every selected crossing is planarized with its geometrically forced local cyclic order, allowing every possible order of multiple crossings along each train.

No candidate of size `0,...,6` passes all three tests in either matching type.

This converts the last support-six exception into a second universal resource payment. If `c_6` is the number of active degree-pure collapsed support-six components and `R_4` is the total number of bounded strict-rhombus contact faces, then internal train sets of distinct relation components are disjoint, so their internal crossing rhombi are disjoint. Hence

\[
\boxed{R_4\ge7c_6,\qquad c_6\le {R_4\over7}.}
\tag{SC.2}
\]

Together with the already proved tangent payment

\[
d_C\ge M_0+2c_6,
\qquad
M_0={b-f\over2}+E_U+p_U,
\]

one obtains the two-currency bound

\[
\boxed{
c_6\le
\min\left\{
{d_C-M_0\over2},
{R_4\over7}
\right\}.
}
\tag{SC.3}
\]

Substitution into the current support accounting gives

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_C
\le
2n_0+n_2+{n_1\over7}+{x_U\over7}
+\min\left\{
{d_C-M_0\over14},
{R_4\over49}
\right\}.
}
\tag{SC.4}
\]

The point is structural. A support-six dual escape now has to spend both contact-tangent capacity and at least seven actual strict rhombi. It is no longer a cheap local closure defect.

---

## 1. Input: the exact 388-cell endpoint

Use the notation of the collapsed rotation-cell reduction. Each internal relation train `e` has a projective direction

\[
0<\alpha_e<\pi,
\qquad
x_e={3\alpha_e\over\pi}.
\]

At each of the six exceptional pentagons, the current exact local geometry becomes the strict integer-difference system

\[
0<G_i<2,
\qquad
2<G_i+G_{i+1}<3.
\tag{1.1}
\]

After enumerating the Eulerian `2`-in/`2`-out relation orientations and the four local order choices at each face, the prior exact certificate leaves

\[
180
\]

labelled cells for the all-cross missing matching type and

\[
208
\]

for the mixed missing matching type. There are 388 in total.

Every actual collapsed support-six block satisfying the current pentagon theorems lies in one of these cells. The present argument works over the whole open rational cell. It does not assume a numerical closure root exists there.

---

## 2. A selected crossing set must satisfy MI.1

There are twelve internal relation trains, hence 66 unordered train pairs. For a pair `e,f`, let

\[
z_{e,f}\in\{0,1\}
\]

record whether their canonical complete train paths cross in a strict rhombus.

For every pair of edge-disjoint simple relation cycles `C,D`, the audited plane-intersection theorem gives

\[
\bigoplus_{e\in E(C),\,f\in E(D)} z_{e,f}
=
\bigoplus_{v\in V(C)\cap V(D)}\eta_v(C,D).
\tag{2.1}
\]

The right side is read exactly from the four-edge cyclic relation order at each common exceptional face. Train pairs sharing an exceptional endpoint remain in the left side; such trains may meet later in a rhombus.

The verifier generates every simple relation cycle and every unordered edge-disjoint cycle pair, then row-reduces (2.1) exactly over `F_2`. For a proposed crossing set `S`, satisfying this affine system is the first necessary test.

Already this rules out `|S|=0` and `|S|=1` in every one of the 388 cells.

---

## 3. Selected crossings must be jointly angle-feasible

A strict unit-rhombus crossing of `e,f` requires

\[
{\pi\over3}<\angle_{\rm proj}(e,f)\le{\pi\over2}.
\]

For representatives `0<\alpha_e,\alpha_f<\pi`, this is equivalent to one of the two oriented strict windows

\[
1<x_e-x_f<2,
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
1<x_f-x_e<2.
\tag{3.1}
\]

The important point is simultaneity. If `S` contains several crossings, one common angle vector must realize all their windows. The coarse regression intentionally forgot this and therefore admitted spurious combinations of crossing bits.

For every MI.1 crossing set `S` with `|S|\le6`, the new verifier enumerates the two orientations in (3.1) for every selected pair and appends all selected inequalities to the same base rotation-cell difference system. Feasibility is decided by the same exact transformed-cycle Bellman--Ford criterion used in the 388-cell theorem.

Unselected train pairs receive no angle constraint. This is an over-relaxation in the safe direction: two trains may have crossing-compatible directions without actually meeting. Only pairs asserted to cross are forced into a crossing window.

---

## 4. The local rotation at a crossing is not free

Angle compatibility and MI.1 are still not enough. A proposed set of train crossings must planarize the fixed exceptional-face rotation system with the correct geometric crossing signs.

This sign is determined by the coherent train orientations.

For a relation train `e`, choose its upper-half-plane unit direction `u_e` and orient the relation edge from the exceptional face carrying the occurrence `+u_e` to the face carrying `-u_e`. Orient the complete train midpoint path from that tail face to that head face.

Along the train, every rhombus is entered through a side whose counterclockwise rhombus boundary vector is `-u_e` and exited through the opposite side `+u_e`. This is true in the first rhombus because the boundary direction reverses across the terminal contact edge, and it propagates across every subsequent shared contact edge.

Suppose trains `e,f` cross and put

\[
D=\det(u_e,u_f)\ne0.
\]

If `D>0`, the crossing rhombus can be written in counterclockwise side order

\[
-u_e,-u_f,+u_e,+u_f.
\]

The oriented midpoint displacement of train `e` is then `-u_f`, while that of train `f` is `+u_e`. If `D<0`, the corresponding displacements are `+u_f` and `-u_e`. In both cases, writing `t_e,t_f` for the oriented train tangents through the crossing,

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{sign}\det(t_e,t_f)=\operatorname{sign}D.}
\tag{4.1}
\]

Now let `eA` denote the dart at the crossing pointing toward the lower-numbered endpoint of relation edge `e`. In the exact cell encoding, `orientation_bits[e]=0` means that lower-numbered endpoint is the tail, while bit `1` means it is the head. Thus

\[
eA=c_e t_e,
\qquad
c_e=
\begin{cases}-1,&\text{bit }e=0,\\+1,&\text{bit }e=1.
\end{cases}
\]

Similarly for `f`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{sign}\det(eA,fA)
=c_ec_f\operatorname{sign}\det(u_e,u_f).
}
\tag{4.2}
\]

Equation (3.1) determines the last sign exactly. Hence the crossing vertex has one forced cyclic order:

- `eA,fA,eB,fB` when the sign in (4.2) is positive;
- `eA,fB,eB,fA` when it is negative.

The two alternating rotations cannot be chosen independently. This is the extra topological information that makes the finite exclusion work.

---

## 5. Exact planarization test

Fix a proposed selected crossing set `S` and one jointly feasible orientation choice for all windows (3.1).

Replace every selected strict-rhombus crossing by a degree-four crossing vertex. Split each of the twelve relation edges at all its selected crossings. At the six original exceptional-face vertices, retain the exact cyclic relation-edge order of the rotation cell. At every crossing vertex, use the forced cyclic order from Section 4.

If one train participates in several selected crossings, their linear order along the complete train is not determined by the coarse rotation cell. The verifier therefore enumerates **every** permutation of those crossings along each train independently. This deliberately gives the proposed drawing all possible topological freedom.

For each resulting rotation system, the verifier counts the face cycles and computes

\[
2-2g=V-E+F.
\tag{5.1}
\]

Any actual planar stopped-train drawing would produce one of these planarizations with

\[
g=0.
\]

Therefore, if every crossing-order permutation has positive genus, the proposed crossing set is impossible globally.

This is only a necessary test. A genus-zero planarization would not by itself prove a straight unit-rhombus realization. The certificate uses the test only in the excluding direction.

---

## 6. Complete enumeration through six crossings

The exact output is:

\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r|r|r}
\text{matching}&|S|&\text{MI.1 sets}&\text{joint-angle sets}&\text{planar sets}\\ \hline
\text{all-cross}&0&0&0&0\\
&1&0&0&0\\
&2&156&132&0\\
&3&1176&96&0\\
&4&10788&756&0\\
&5&87648&1416&0\\
&6&583296&5160&0\\ \hline
\text{mixed}&0&0&0&0\\
&1&0&0&0\\
&2&40&32&0\\
&3&696&192&0\\
&4&10024&528&0\\
&5&82264&1288&0\\
&6&617952&5480&0
\end{array}
\tag{6.1}
\]

Here:

- `MI.1 sets` counts selected train-pair sets satisfying all cycle-intersection equations;
- `joint-angle sets` counts those for which at least one common projective-angle vector lies in the base rotation cell and in every selected crossing window;
- `planar sets` counts those for which at least one jointly feasible crossing orientation and at least one ordering of crossings along every train gives genus zero with the forced crossing rotations.

The final column is identically zero for every `|S|\le6` in both matching types.

Every actual global collapsed block supplies exactly such a selected set, namely its actual internal strict-rhombus crossings. Therefore no actual block can have at most six internal crossings, proving (SC.1).

The certificate uses only integer difference constraints, bit arithmetic over `F_2`, finite permutation enumeration, and Euler characteristic. No floating-point decision occurs.

---

## 7. Global rhombus payment

Let `K,K'` be distinct one-anchor relation components. Their internal relation-train sets are disjoint: a complete relation train has only its two terminal exceptional incidences and therefore cannot be an internal edge of both components.

A strict rhombus belongs to exactly two complete trains. Hence a rhombus counted by `X_K`, whose two crossing trains are internal to `K`, cannot simultaneously be counted by `X_{K'}`.

Summing (SC.1) over all degree-pure collapsed support-six components gives

\[
R_4
\ge
\sum_K X_K
\ge
7c_6,
\]

which is (SC.2).

The preceding tangent-payment theorem independently gives

\[
c_6\le{d_C-M_0\over2}.
\]

Taking the minimum proves (SC.3). Inserting `c_6/7` into the existing support count proves (SC.4).

No claim is made here that (SC.4), by itself, beats every existing scalar frontier after all other variables are eliminated. It is a new exact coupling: a support-six component must pay simultaneously in full contact tangent and in actual strict-rhombus population.

---

## 8. Remaining obligation

This theorem does not exclude the collapsed support-six mechanism outright. It proves that any surviving block is globally expensive:

\[
\boxed{X_K\ge7.}
\]

The direct finite search becomes less attractive beyond six crossings because the number of selected train-pair sets grows quickly. The next useful attacks are therefore:

1. extract a structural invariant from the forced crossing rotations that replaces weight-by-weight enumeration;
2. feed the new `R_4\ge7c_6` payment into the live terminal/FREE rhombus and train budgets; and
3. only if needed, enumerate seven-crossing endpoint types with symmetry and crossing-order pruning before applying the nonlinear six-face closure equations.

**Disposition:** proved an exact seven-rhombus floor for every global degree-pure collapsed support-six block and coupled the last support-six exception to both contact-tangent dimension and strict-rhombus population. C-II remains open.
